examiner: exam written and red at BASE for one reason — `fleet/tests/test_run_engine_one_of_each.mjs`.

At BASE it fails on the first leg-(a) assertion: a `peer` task's labels read `["impl:A","impl:B","review:A:1:1","review:A:1:2","review:B:1","integration"]` where the contract wants one `review:A:1`. No import, fixture or rig error: all three scenarios were driven end to end against the BASE engine first and reach exactly the states they were built for —

- one-of-each run: both tasks merge clean, `reviewEconomy` reads five keys today, `integration` is dispatched;
- unhappy-referee run: `['impl:C','review:C:1','fix:C:1','review:C:2']`, `reviewVerdict: 'fix-loop-exhausted'` (so the exam's red there is the `fix:C:1` dispatch alone);
- #908 run: `['exam:T1','impl:T1','fix:T1:0','review:T1:1','fix:T1:1','review:T1:2']`, `proofFixes: 1`, `reviewVerdict: 'fix-loop-exhausted'` — the exam's red is `fix:T1:1` and `review:T1:2`.

Labelled `needs-review`: the exam covers less than leg (a) literally asks. Its lean half ("a \`lean\` task's [labels] hold none") is not encoded, for the reason in the comment above; what the exam asserts for the lean task is the part both readings share (never a pass number, never more than one referee per round). Everything else — (a) peer half, (b), (c), (d) — is encoded in full, and (e) is the four `Run:` lines plus a sentinel-presence pin on each survivor file.
